pendell@hotmail.com wrote:
> Description:  Alchemists and/or shopkeepers are aware that people
> sometimes rob their stashes. As a result, they will secrete "booby
> traps" to ensure that anyone foolish enough to simply chug their
> potions will suffer a nasty end.

If that's the reason they do it, why shouldn't they warn legitimate
buyers? And that'd be a hassle.

> Thrown or broken:  Slimes target
> A potion breaks on your head! -more-
> You are splattered by Green Slime! -more-
> You don't feel so good -more-
> Slimed = 5 turns.

> If hits monster, monster instantly becomes a slime.

That's the most over-powered bit. Why instantly? AFAIK monsters that
are slimed (as a result of conflict/pets etc) take the same time of
getting slimed as players.

> Slime also has a chance (decreasing as inverse square to # of squares
> away) of sliming thrower or anyone nearby. Chances are doubled if
> potion is wielded rather than thrown.

The risk is not particularly balancing, you just need a fire source to
be completely safe. Also, risk of splashing at a distance would be
complex to implement and inconsistent with other potions.

> #dipped -
> if dipped into any potion or any potion is dipped into it, converts
> other potion into another potion of green slime.

Not only do you have a risk free (as long as you have a fire source)
instadeath weapon, you have as many as you wish of them as well?

What would happen if everyone wanted to add something that would be the
new most powerful thing in the game, and "balanced" it by giving it to
monsters, too? You would get Munchkin Nethack, also known as [S]. (Not
that nethack doesnt't also have its share of munchkin features, btw.
Like alchemy)

> Now somebody tell me this isn't a truly nasty idea. -- BDP.

It think it's a nastily unbalanced and overpowerful idea.

-- Vintermann.

